    <p>Azoth is the first open-source AI model for general malware
    detection. It classifies samples from <a href="/cleave/">cleave</a>'s
    capability output, covering ELF, PE, Mach-O, Java .class, Python
    .pyc, and compiled AppleScript, plus source-code ecosystems like
    Python, npm, and VS Code extensions.</p>

    <p>It runs locally on the host calling it. No network round-trips, no
    telemetry. <a href="/litmus/">litmus</a> is the reference scanner,
    but azoth isn't bound to it — the weights, the training pipeline, and
    the capability schema are Apache 2.0, so any tool can drive the model
    or retrain it on its own data.</p>

    <h2>Why a small, dedicated model</h2>

    <p><strong>Cost.</strong> Per-token pricing doesn't pencil out for
    continuous scanning. Every dependency update, every CI run, every
    artifact in a registry is another classification, and the metered
    models get expensive fast at fleet scale. Azoth runs on CPU with no
    per-call charge.</p>

    <p><strong>Footprint.</strong> No GPU, no accelerator. The training
    pipeline distills from analyst-labeled malware corpora, public
    threat reports, and frontier LLMs into a model that fits on a
    Raspberry Pi. The same weights run on a laptop, a CI runner, or an
    endpoint agent.</p>

    <p><strong>Format-agnostic input.</strong> Azoth classifies on
    cleave's structured capabilities, not raw bytes or disassembly
    tokens. One model covers every format cleave decomposes, and as
    long as cleave still resolves the capabilities, surface-level
    changes — minor recompiles, string mangling — don't perturb the
    feature vector. The trade-off is that azoth inherits cleave's
    evasion surface: anything that defeats cleave's unpacking, dynamic
    loader handling, or control-flow analysis is invisible to the
    model.</p>

    <p><strong>License.</strong> Apache 2.0 on the weights, the training
    pipeline, and the runtime — same as the rest of atomdrift.</p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>In active development. Initial weights, evaluation results, and
    the training pipeline will land on
    <a href="https://codeberg.org/atomdrift" target="_blank">Codeberg</a>
    with the first release. Until then, treat anything on this page as
    the design intent — not a benchmark.</p>

